<!--
  What this app is allowed to talk to, and why each clause is here.

  ⚠ **In no Tauri template, and that is the hazard rather than the comfort.**
  `tauri android init` does not write this file, so it survives one intact —
  but `init` does rewrite `AndroidManifest.xml`, and
  `android:networkSecurityConfig` there is the only thing that reaches this
  file at all. An `init` re-run therefore leaves it on disk, pointed at by
  nothing, with every symptom below back. `nativecheck` asserts the attribute
  and this file as a **pair**, against comment-stripped source, because the
  paragraph below quotes the very element it is about.

  ⚠ Cleartext is permitted, and it is not laziness. `config.rs`'s
  `normalize_origin` keeps `http://` and `https://` as *different trust
  boundaries* rather than upgrading one to the other, because a self-hosted
  control plane on a LAN, a dev stand, and `local.rs`'s `http://127.0.0.1` daemon
  route are all ordinary shapes for this software. Android blocks cleartext by
  default from API 28 up, so without this file every one of them fails with a
  message about the server being down.

  This file is the whole policy, for every build type. android:usesCleartextTraffic
  used to sit on the manifest's application element with a per build type
  placeholder and decided nothing: the platform ignores it from API 24 up wherever
  a network security config is present, and minSdk is 24. It is deleted, and the
  manifest says why.

  ⚠ `<certificates src="user" />` is the half that decides whether a private CA
  works at all. `reqwest` resolves to rustls with `rustls-platform-verifier` on
  this platform, which calls Android's own trust manager over JNI — so this one
  file governs the `/v1` leg and the webview's relay and daemon legs alike. Drop
  the `user` entry and a control plane behind a CA somebody installed on their own
  phone is refused, while the same URL works in Chrome.

  ⚠ It sits in `<base-config>`, which means every origin and every build type,
  and those are **two** decisions rather than one. Only the first of them is
  forced.

  ⚠ **By origin there is genuinely no narrower place.** `<domain-config>` is
  the mechanism for scoping a trust anchor and it takes literal hostnames at build
  time. This app has none: `REEMOAT_DEFAULT_SERVER` is a fork's compile time seed
  and is unset in this repository, the address is otherwise typed into the server
  picker at run time, and the relay is learned per machine from
  `POST /v1/tokens` — two machines of one account legitimately sit on different
  relays. There is no set of hostnames to enumerate here, and one invented at
  build time would be precisely the self hosted deployment this clause exists for,
  broken.

  ⚠ **By build type there is one, and it is deliberately not taken.**
  `<debug-overrides>` carries trust anchors and needs no second resource file, so
  a `<debug-overrides><trust-anchors><certificates src="user" /></trust-anchors>`
  block would scope the widening to debuggable builds with one edit to this file
  and nothing else. It is not done, and that is the **owner's call** rather than a
  measurement: a private CA is the ordinary shape for a self hosted control plane,
  and somebody installing a release build and pointing it at their own box behind
  their own CA is the deployment this software exists for. What keeping it costs
  is named in full below, leg by leg, and Q7.144 is where the decision was argued.

  ⚠ **The cleartext argument in `AndroidManifest.xml` does not cover this
  one.** That paragraph is about `cleartextTrafficPermitted`, which really has
  nowhere narrower to go — `<debug-overrides>` has no such attribute at all, so
  expressing a per build type cleartext policy needs a second resource file under
  `src/debug`. The two clauses share `<base-config>` for different reasons and only
  that one is actually stuck. Reading them as one argument makes the anchor look
  forced when it is chosen.

  So what it loosens, named rather than implied. All three legs, because somebody
  who installs a hostile CA profile gets all three:

    1. `proxy.rs`'s `/v1` calls, which carry the account's bearer credential in an
       authorization header. This is the sharpest one: a CA that intercepts it
       reads and can replay that credential against the control plane. Nothing
       under the TLS layer protects it — it is an ordinary HTTPS API call.
    2. The webview's relay leg. The bytes inside it are a Noise_IK session keyed
       on the device key and the daemon's announced machine key, so an
       intercepting CA sees relay framing and ciphertext it holds no key for and
       cannot reach a session's contents. What it does see is who is talking to
       which machine, and when.
    3. The webview's daemon leg. Over the relay this is case 2. Over a LAN or a
       loopback address it is cleartext by the clause above, which a user CA is
       not needed to read at all.

  The honest summary is that this clause trades leg 1 for the private CA
  deployment, that legs 2 and 3 are already end to end encrypted underneath it,
  and that the mitigation is the one Android already has: a user CA is an
  explicit, per device install that the system warns about persistently.
  `docs/DECISIONS.md` Q7.144 is the argument and what was tried instead.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
